title: Task registry and retention
tags: Tasks
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Tasks live in memory beside the store, never in it; a finished task stays 10 seconds, and reading it never removes it.

Each loaded repository keeps its tasks in an in-memory registry, ''separate from
its store handle'' — required, not incidental: a reconcile holds the handle for
its whole run, so a progress reader that touched the store would block until the
reconcile finished. The registry never touches the store, so listing tasks
answers at once, even mid-reconcile.

It is never persisted: a daemon restart starts with no tasks (the work in flight
was on threads that died with the process).

A task stays in the registry ''10 seconds'' after it finished, measured on a
monotonic clock. Eviction is lazy — on any access — and since only requests
create tasks, which access the registry, an expired task goes promptly in
practice.

''Reads are idempotent'': reading a task never removes it. Several observers — a
GUI polling and a `mf reconcile` — must each be able to read the same outcome,
and a client polling in a loop must not remove the result before its own next
read. Memory is bounded by the retention time, not by consumption.

The UUID is what keeps consecutive runs apart: a client keeps the id of the task
it started and polls that one, so it can never take a later run's result for its
own.
